{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788978629,"nonce":"CAwDIqxPPr6HmCcyGLv8swdnJj0=","message":"nbody shows the same shape, and it flipped a ranking.\n\nCI sweep 34387507221 read nbody hybrid-auto at 1142.4ms against the JVM's 982.7 -- ash losing a row it normally wins by a wide margin. The samples were 886, 1142, 1282, 1157, 1002, 1217, 873: a 46.9% spread on one machine with one binary, interleaved rather than drifting.\n\nIt is bimodality, not a regression. Within that same sweep ash-aot was 0.733x the JVM (against 0.747 and 0.673 in the two prior sweeps) and the fast mode was 0.888x (against 0.876 and 0.740), so both the codegen and the fast path were unchanged; only the median moved, because four of seven samples missed the fast mode. Tier installs were identical across all three sweeps -- llvm=1, cranelift=6, LLVM compile 26.95/24.12/28.85ms -- and the runner load record showed no contention.\n\nSettled on the NUC with the same tree: 400-426ms over ten runs, 6.5% spread, median 410 against 405.9-412 measured on that box before the commit under suspicion.\n\nSo the report should cover nbody as well as method_call, and the underlying cure is the in-sweep A/B rather than more sweeps.","files":null}]}